What you could... What we could do, like, you know. Yeah. As I say, it was paid for what you did, like. That was quite a good system, was it? Yes, yes. Well, it's just handy. You know, we didn't have babysitters. Well, I didn't. So you were able to keep up as well with your craft and do as well. Yes, yes. Keep in with that. Right. OK. Good. And when did you come back then, full-time? Once my daughter started full-time.

66
00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:45.460
I did part-time then, nine till three. So did they have shift work here at all? No. Or was it all the same? No. They did. I didn't do the shift work, but how long it went on for, I don't know. That was on the knitting. There wasn't night shift at all? No, no, no, just the afternoons. OK, so you came back for part-time while the children were in school, really? Yes, yes. And there were others then working on till...?

67
00:31:45.460 --> 00:32:14.770
Five or half a cent? Yeah, 20 plus five, so. OK, all right, fine, good, good. You know, was there a shop associated with the factory from the beginning? Were you allowed to buy? Did you have any perks from working here at all? Were you allowed to buy socks here or something? Oh, yes, yes, we could. You know, just, there wasn't a shop, but whatever, we could see, you know, we used to go and ask one of the girls. We used to keep them in boxes then on shelves, you know.

68
00:32:14.770 --> 00:32:43.650
Out in the factory. And if we wanted something, we'd go and ask. You could buy... Yes, yes, seconds mostly. Yeah, right. Was there anything in some factories, some clothes used to pilfer some things? Was that a problem here at all? Once, I think. Somebody's pay packet. But I think they knew who had done it. And I remember the Mr Jones saying...

69
00:32:43.650 --> 00:33:11.230
If you're short of money, you come to ask me. You don't pinch of me. If you want something, I'll help you. But the girls didn't use to sort of take some socks home? No, no, no. There's no tradition of anything like that yet. No, not that I know of, no. There are in some, I think there is in some factories. Yeah, yeah. You know, you talked about the holidays now. Before you went on holidays, was that, did you have a good time the day before? Was that a sort of celebration that you weren't going to? No, no.

70
00:33:11.230 --> 00:33:40.900
No. You wouldn't allow them to have a bit of a jamboree before Christmas? Oh, we used to go from, once we finished work, we used to go then out from work. They didn't decorate the factory, did they, at all? Yes. Yes. We used to all decorate. All trimmings hanging. You can't do it now because of the electric and things, isn't it? But we used to, from the lights, we used to have trimmings everywhere, Christmas tree, but nothing now.

71
00:33:40.900 --> 00:34:14.720
And did you bring wine bottles in or bottles of drink at all? I forgot about this. If it was cold, yeah. And we used to bring some whiskey in because it was so cold. We used to have a break. We used to have a drop of whiskey and tea so that it would warm us up to come out. And this was during any working day then? It was very cold in winter. Oh, yes, very cold, yeah.

72
00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:46.560
Very cold. Because sometimes, you know, we had some quite heavy snows, didn't we? Yes, yes. The bosses didn't know, mind. The bosses didn't. Am I supposed to say? Yes, it's all right, don't worry, we won't have a sack. I don't think so. No, no. I don't think they'll sack me now. And on a Friday then, if it was holiday time, like Christmas, we used to, some of the girls used to do their own brew.

73
00:34:46.850 --> 00:35:17.090
And we'd start at 10 o'clock in the morning then, and we were finished then. This was before Christmas, is it? Friday before Christmas. You had a party, you said. Did you go out as a group then for a party? Yes, yeah. Oh, they used to run trips, you know, to have a lunch. OK. Did you go on trips otherwise at all, you know, like sort of to visit places at all? No, we used to go strawberry picking on a Saturday with the children.

74
00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:44.140
I put a bus on and used to go to Heriford. Any other trips at all? I know before I came here they used to go to Wilfracombe and all those places but I hadn't started here then. Was there a social club associated with the factory? No, no. So there's no facilities like table tennis or anything like that or part of the factory? No, no.

75
00:35:44.140 --> 00:36:09.730
How many women would you say were working here when there was most, you know, at its busiest of all? Hundred. How many are here now? Sixty-something, I know. Right, so it went down for a period, did it? Yes, yes. Were you aware that there were problems at one time? Well, on the sock side of it, the hand socks, you know, the old ones, there's only two girls working on them now out of a hundred.

76
00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:32.770
And they're still wool, aren't they? Yeah, they're so expensive. Yeah, they're high-end, aren't they? Very, very expensive. Hot socks and things, they still buy them. But they're a lot of money. Did you have a party for the children? Christmas time, was that organised as part of the... No, no, we didn't have any parties for the children.

77
00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:59.340
So have any of the family followed you in the tradition of working here at all? No. How did you see your wage? Did you think of it as when you were married now? Did you think of it as the main wage earner or were you extra money? Extra money. Extra money for me. So what did you spend your money of when you had the children? Well, we bought the house so we were saving to do it up.

78
00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:30.430
So on really own domestic things? Yes, it just went. To improve quality of life, really. Right, okay, fine. You were talking about the toilets, I'm going to the toilet here. What were the facilities like in the early years? Were they clean and tidy? Oh, they were clean, but they were very old-fashioned. Was there somebody coming in to clean? Yes, yes, we had a cleaner. And the factory itself as well? Yes, that was kept clean. It used to polish the floor.

79
00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:56.800
And were you allowed to smoke when you were working? Oh, no, no. I was smoking. If you wanted to smoke, I don't know if you smoke at all. No, I don't smoke at all. They used to go to the toilet to smoke. So that's why you was watching that they didn't stay in long, is it? More than likely. More than likely. And now they've got to go outside, have they? Got to go outside, no. Okay, that's changed as well, hasn't it? Yes.

80
00:37:57.090 --> 00:38:33.950
Do you think in this factory that as workers you were treated fairly? When Mr Jones was here, yes. Okay. All right. Did you sort of look up to the foreman? Was he sort of special or were you just aware that he was terrified? We were terrified of him. Was that because he was very strict and unsmiling? I don't know. He just, you know.

81
00:38:33.950 --> 00:39:01.010
I got on all right to them, but, you know, I got on with my work, you know, but used to come round on a Friday then with a bag for sweets. Used to go round us all. And give you sweets? No, he wanted sweets. He wanted sweets? He wanted to provide sweets? Yeah, yeah. But as I say, I got on all right to them, you know. If somebody didn't do their work properly, what would happen then?

82
00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:29.920
Or Mr Jones would have him in the office to tell him. And do you remember any people being sacked or...? No, no. There were a lot, a lot of them were older than, you know, than me, I said myself. So you were quite young, one of the younger ones working here? Yes, one of two of us. Right, OK. Were there somebody else who started at the same time as you or...? Yeah, two of us, I think, started.

83
00:39:30.400 --> 00:40:07.070
And did you both stay on then for a long time? No, I don't think the other girl did. OK. Right. When you started here, there were very few men working here, you said. Yes. Has that changed in the time you've been here? Oh, yes. Quite a lot. What is the ratio now about how many men to women? Oh, well... Just an idea. See, there's... I think there's about 10 or 11 working here. Are they treated? Are they teased?

84
00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:36.210
How are they treated? They're in another part of the factory. We have got three out here at the moment, but they're pressing, but they're on their own over in there. OK. And there's no sort of teasing or...? Oh, yes. Oh, yes, there's a bit of teasing. And there's a young... Well, he's not young, he's a disabled chap. Come from Rempoi. He's been here a few months now. But... Oh, yes, the boys are teased.

85
00:40:36.210 --> 00:41:05.630
Are they? In what way then? I don't hear them. There's young girls out there now, isn't there? So I don't know. What do they say? Can you give an example? No, I don't hear it because I'm this side. I know they're being teased because you can hear them laughing. I'm over this side, sir. What about the language on the factory floor? Because in some factories there were menus quite...

86
00:41:05.630 --> 00:41:34.940
Rough language at times? Would you say not at all? No. Oh, one or two, if something's gone really wrong. But very rare. Was it about Welsh? Were there Welsh speakers here? There is Welsh speakers, yes. And they could speak Welsh, no problem? No, yes. You know, I understand Welsh. I don't speak Welsh, but I understand it. And I answer them in English if they speak Welsh. So there's no problem with speaking Welsh? No, no.

87
00:41:35.710 --> 00:42:13.410
Back now, if you can think back over the years you've worked here, what would you say the things that stay in your memory, you know, when you look back? I think it was just the company and, you know, we used to have such fun, you know, and everybody helped each other in those days, you know? If somebody was getting married, did you give presents to them? Oh, yes, they do that now.

88
00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:43.630
Yes. Was that a collection? We have a collection and, you know. There was no tradition of them having to come into the factory in their wedding clothes or anything like that? I used to dress them up. We used to dress them up. Tell us about that now then. You know, same thing. Just before getting married, is it? Getting married, put a veil on them and dress them up in the wool. Or they used to sew up their coats. You know, they could put their arms in.

89
00:42:43.630 --> 00:43:07.900
They were sewn up. Well, the factory buses used to be outside picking them up. Well, of course, we all had to run. Well, I didn't, but the girls had to run because they used to just jump on and the buses used to go. It wouldn't wait for anybody. Of course, they were trying to get their sleeves, hands into the sleeves, but they couldn't. So they were all dressed, makeup on.

90
00:43:07.900 --> 00:43:15.100
And what did they do to them? Dress them in what then? In a veil and same thing, tie wool.

91
00:43:15.230 --> 00:43:41.090
Wool was everything then because, you know. That's what you had, isn't it? That's what we had, you know. Did they have to do anything in the factory, you know, like mop-up or...? No. No, you didn't do anything, I thought. No, no, no. No, we had to tidy up after us anyway, you know. So you sewed the sleeves. And the coat itself or just the sleeves? No, just the sleeves. Did they know to expect this? Well, in the end they did, but no, what else?

92
00:43:44.000 --> 00:44:13.340
They had factory buses then, you said there. Yes, double-decker buses waiting outside. Was that for all the factories? Well, there was Pullmans. There was a big fact, Pullmans, Tenderton and Corgi. Because they used to come from the valleys. So what valleys were they then? Tigwaith, Bryn Amman. Right. They came down here from the whole of the Amman Valley then? Oh, yes, yes. Did you notice there were sort of differences between different people who came from the different areas?

93
00:44:13.340 --> 00:44:42.560
No, not really. We used to, you know, everybody joined in. We used to have some fun. There wasn't a lot, really. Not in Corgi, anyway. But they came from, so these buses would serve the whole part of the factory? Yes, yes. Right. What about down towards, you know, Kamar then? Pentegross. OK. I'm not sure about croissants. I can't think of anywhere else. Because we've got Joyce now, she's retired, but she's come back to help us.

94
00:44:42.620 --> 00:45:13.570
She's from Tye Gwaif. And then we've got Laura. She's from Bryn Amman. It's very important, really, for the whole community, isn't it, these factories? Yes, yes. So when you look back, then, it's a sort of camaraderie with the girls that you remember, is it? Yes. And was the wage important as well? Well... Did that go up? Do you remember? Yes. When I got married, I was earning 12.60 an hour. A week, sorry.

95
00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:43.900
12.60 I was having a week. And then, do you remember what it went up to then after that? Well, no, it crept up a bit. It's not that big now, mind. Minimal wage we're having now, some of us. What about when you were working at home? Do you remember how much that would have brought you in? That would have been quite difficult. I suppose it varied, did it? It varied, yes. It all depends if the children were behaving, isn't it?

96
00:45:43.900 --> 00:46:10.180
But I gotta say, they are very good, even Mr. Jones. If anything went wrong with your family, you could go home and, you know. So if you wanted time off for something like a funeral or something, would that be all right? Yeah, yes. You could make up your hours. If you want to make up your hours, it's up to you. But when my husband died, they were marvellous, you know?

97
00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:39.390
So if somebody retired then, did they have a due for that? Oh yes, we've had a due. We're having a due now at the end of this month. Were they given a gift of any kind? Have you had a gift you've worked for for so many years? No? Not as gold watch for all these years of service? No, no. Might have a pullover perhaps. And do they give something to somebody who's retiring? Yes, a pullover.

98
00:46:39.390 --> 00:47:11.970
Or whatever. A pullover. A pullover. A cardigan or a throw for a belt, which Mary had. She's just had it, Mary, one of the girls that was in school with me. The girls on the floor clicked. You know, for my 60th, they gave me money towards... OK. Well, they gave me money and said, you can do what you like with it. So all my family, I didn't need anything, so they gave me money.

99
00:47:11.970 --> 00:47:22.620
So I put everything in to have in a ring like, you know. The company itself doesn't give anything for a service. No, a bunch of flowers.

100
00:47:23.460 --> 00:47:50.990
Okay. And do you know the women who've left then? Do they keep in touch? Is there sort of like a club that they meet? Well, yeah, no, no. Or do you meet up with, you know, people who've left? Because you've been here so long. Yeah. We've got this due now, September, the end of the month. Christopher's paying for us to go to have an Indian. Right. So Joyce is coming. The one that's come back to help. And Mary, now I'll give her a ring.

101
00:47:50.990 --> 00:48:20.700
She used to be supervisor down the other end. She'd come and meet us. So there'd be a group of how many of you then? Well, whoever wants to come from the factory. Right, OK. And did they used to pay in the old days for the actual Christmas party you went out? Yes. They did? Yeah, yeah. Fine. Oh, we have a Christmas party now, mind. And they pay for everything, the drinks and the food. So is that a dinner somewhere? A dinner, yes. A dinner dance or something? No, just dinner. Right.

102
00:48:21.310 --> 00:48:55.520
You know, when you were talking about the singing here, was that to help you with the work or was it just to enjoy? To enjoy, I think, you know? Yeah. Yeah. Did you still, you don't still sing here? Nothing. No. We're not allowed radios. Well, we are, but they need, the radio company, they need £1,000 a year. You know, like you pay for your television. So you had a record player playing then? Yes. And so you bought... We used to have a wireless.

103
00:48:55.520 --> 00:49:19.730
until they said that we've got to pay a thousand pounds a year for the company to license for us. Yeah, license for us to. So the girls have got earphones now. They've got a little old personal. So nobody talks now and they just listen to the... I can't stand it, mind all the time in my youth. When the music was playing, you'd have to...

104
00:49:19.730 --> 00:49:50.370
records that you enjoyed there and you could choose your own records. Did you choose a record at all? Yes, well we all had, we all used to pull in but you know one person every week used to have a choice on a record. Which ones did you like? I liked Cliff Richards, I did. Right, if you had to go back again though would you choose to come back to work in the factory? Yes, I've enjoyed myself, yeah, I've got to say.

105
00:49:50.660 --> 00:50:14.780
And there's been no break, except when you left for the children, there's been no time when the factory's closed down or anything. No, no, no. Employment has been constant. Yes, constant. They've been into liquidation once or twice, but, you know, we've worked until Pounder Bayer came in and then Corgi bought it back.

106
00:50:16.420 --> 00:50:47.300
And I believe there's been some interest, royal family interest here as well. Oh, yes, yes. Do you remember their visits at all? Oh, yes. Princess Sam, she was very nice, she was. When did she come? Do you remember where? No. How many years ago with that being? Oh, they've burned because there's been asbestos, yeah. They've thrown a lot of stuff. I can't see now. This lady, Pam, I used to burn with her.

107
00:50:47.300 --> 00:51:19.740
She went to Australia, America. Ah, right. She's down here now. So, Princess Anne came. Did you have to demonstrate anything to her? Were you demonstrating? Yeah, they opened the new factory. Ah, right. So, she came to open it. And anyone else at all? Any other royal family visitor? Prince Charles. When he goes to Motherfine, he comes, falls here sometimes.

108
00:51:20.770 --> 00:51:52.580
I think he wears your socks, I think, does he? Yes, and I've downed his socks. Oh, did you do any tanning then, you know? Yeah, he's got hunting socks. Sorry? He's got hunting socks. Are they made here? No, but he has sent them here for us to repair for him. We would have a shock. About anyway, did you do any tanning anyway? I do all repairs. What do you mean by repairs now? Well, if companies send something back. You know, I...

109
00:51:52.700 --> 00:52:29.890
So on the machine or by hand? No, by hand. I sit down, I haven't got a machine job or anything. I just fix, you know, how can I say if there's a... Sometimes... Obviously the work has changed in the time you've been here, but you said you also worked as a sort of teacher on the structure as well. Yes, yes, on the knitting. And what did you do there?

110
00:52:30.180 --> 00:52:57.630
You just told the young girls sat with them? No, we have to stand up because the machines are quite big. I don't know if you... No, no. But you used to be awake standing or sitting? Sitting now, but it used to be standing. All day? All day. The girls stand all day on the knitting. And is that what you had to do when you were doing the socks? No, not the socks sitting down, but the knitting, you stand up. This is the knitwear then? Yes.

111
00:52:57.630 --> 00:53:26.000
Yeah, it's chunky. That can be quite tiring then, can it? Yes, it's hard going. So if you were doing, when did the sort of knitwear come in? Do you have any idea what time that happened? No. No, when did it? Right, so when did you start doing the knitwear now then? Let's get that down on the tape. 1960. Okay, and that was a completely different... To the socks. Yeah. How did you learn to do that? How?

112
00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:56.450
He taught you himself, did he? Yes, yes. And what were you doing at the beginning? Was it... Socks, socks. Socks. So what were you doing with the knitwear at the beginning? Was it jumpers? No, no, socks. Oh, socks. The socks were flat. Right. And then... I haven't got one yet to show you. We used to then... Bring them forward. No, they used to pick them up on the sock machine after we'd done the leg. And then they used to...

113
00:53:56.830 --> 00:54:28.740
bring them around and link them up at the back. Quite a few different techniques. Yes, yes. So you were talking then about what you call it when there's different gauges, what are they that you use here, the different, is it gauges? Yes, there's fine gauge and there's heavy gauge. And which ones did you do the socks in? Whatever. Ah, right, it could be either. Could be either, yeah. Right, OK. So to sum up, is there anything I've left out now that you want to tell me about the...

114
00:54:28.740 --> 00:54:42.385
History of the factory or any stories that you want to say? I think I've said all the naughty things. So, okay. Well, thank you very much. We'll draw it to an end.

